# ─── Database (Supabase PostgreSQL) ───
# CRITICAL: keep connection_limit=10 (or higher) and pool_timeout=20.
# With pgbouncer=true, Prisma's DEFAULT connection_limit is 1 — which
# makes every concurrent request time out ("Timed out fetching a new
# connection from the connection pool") and cascades into fleet-wide
# DATABASE_ERROR 500s. Do NOT copy a connection_limit=1 value here.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=10&pool_timeout=20"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres"

# ─── Supabase (Storage + Auth) ───
SUPABASE_URL="https://YOUR_PROJECT.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="your-service-role-key"

# ─── Authentication Secrets ───
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('hex'))"
JWT_SECRET="CHANGE_ME_generate_random_64_char_hex"
SESSION_SECRET="CHANGE_ME_generate_random_64_char_hex"

# ─── Required MFA policy (SEC-008, 2026-09-04) ───
# Optional. Leave UNSET in normal operation.
#
# Every SUPER_ADMIN / DISTRICT_ADMIN / SCHOOL_ADMIN, and anyone holding
# `canTriggerPanic`, must complete TOTP enrollment before the API issues them a
# full session. Password alone must not be able to fire a district-wide
# lockdown. Enforcement is ADVISORY until a deadline and BLOCKING after it; the
# deadline is a dated constant in apps/api/src/auth/mfa-policy.ts
# (2026-10-04T00:00:00.000Z), NOT a default of this variable — so a deployment
# that never sets anything still switches on, on the shipped date. There is no
# "forever grace" state a forgotten variable can produce.
#
# Accepted values:
#   <ISO-8601 instant>   move the deadline (e.g. 2026-12-01T00:00:00Z)
#   now | immediate      enforce with no grace
#   off | never | false  BREAK-GLASS: turn the derived policy off platform-wide
#
# BREAK-GLASS notes: it is SUBTRACTIVE ONLY — an explicit per-user
# `User.mfaRequired` still blocks — it logs a loud warning on first use, and it
# takes a redeploy, so nobody holding a session can flip it. A value that is
# neither an instant nor a listed keyword falls back to the built-in deadline,
# never to "off": a typo must not silently downgrade a life-safety platform.
#
# A user who loses their authenticator uses their backup codes. There is
# deliberately no "reset someone's MFA" endpoint.
# MFA_REQUIRED_ENFORCE_AFTER=

# ─── WebSocket / Device Secrets ───
DEVICE_SECRET_KEY="CHANGE_ME_generate_random_64_char_hex"
DEVICE_JWT_SECRET="CHANGE_ME_generate_random_64_char_hex"

# ─── Redis (required for realtime + token revocation) ───
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# ─── CORS ───
# REQUIRED in production. Comma-separated list of allowed frontend origins
# (no trailing slash). Without this set, the API refuses to boot in
# production — we used to fall back to a .vercel.app wildcard which let
# any Vercel-hosted page make authenticated cross-origin requests.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://your-app.vercel.app"

# ─── Trusted proxy hop count (security wave 2026-08-01, finding ACC-08) ───
# How many trusted proxies APPEND to X-Forwarded-For in front of this API.
# apps/api/src/security/client-ip.ts picks the client IP by counting this many
# entries FROM THE RIGHT: with N trusted appenders the real client sits at
# index (len - N), and every entry to its left is client-supplied and ignored.
#
# That single value is the key for every per-IP rate limit AND the ipAddress
# recorded in AuditLog / RequestLog / Screen — including the forensic record of
# who triggered a district-wide lockdown. Treat it as a security setting.
#
# DEFAULT IS 2 because that is the measured Railway chain — the Railway edge
# plus one internal hop, i.e. two appenders. Observed in production:
#   x-forwarded-for: "216.241.83.102, 152.233.76.9"
#                     ^ real client   ^ internal hop (rotates per request)
#
# RE-VERIFY AGAINST A REAL REQUEST'S X-Forwarded-For WHENEVER THE CHAIN
# CHANGES. Putting Cloudflare or a WAF in front adds an appender; running
# without the proxy chain removes them. A wrong value does not crash anything,
# it silently selects the wrong entry:
#   TOO HIGH → you select a position the CLIENT controls, so an attacker can
#              send their own X-Forwarded-For to rotate the throttle key at
#              will (defeating every brute-force cap) and to forge the audit IP.
#   TOO LOW  → you select a rotating internal hop, so the throttle key moves on
#              every request and rate limits never fire. That was the
#              2026-07-07 bug.
# Must be an integer >= 1; anything else falls back to the default. A chain
# SHORTER than this count clamps to the leftmost entry (local dev, direct hits,
# in-cluster probes) — that case is spoofable, so it must not be the production
# shape. Leave unset on Railway.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=2

# Shared secret for the PLAYER'S SAME-ORIGIN GATEWAY (2026-09-02, P0-1).
# Set the SAME value on BOTH services (Vercel web + Railway API), or leave it
# unset on both.
#
# Why it exists: some OEM Android WebViews load the player shell from the web
# origin but cannot reach the API origin at all (TLS chain / DNS / proxy) — the
# screen sits on "Connecting to your CMS…" forever. Such a device falls back to
# running its whole control plane through Next middleware on the web origin
# (apps/web/src/middleware.ts). That hop appends VERCEL'S egress address to
# X-Forwarded-For, so without this secret every gateway device in the fleet
# would resolve to ONE client IP: a single shared throttle key (per-IP
# brute-force caps collapse) and an AuditLog `ipAddress` naming Vercel instead
# of whoever fired a district-wide lockdown.
# The gateway therefore forwards the real client IP in `x-venueos-gw-client-ip`
# alongside this secret in `x-venueos-gw-secret`; the API honors the forwarded
# IP ONLY on a constant-time match (apps/api/src/security/client-ip.ts) and
# otherwise falls back to its normal right-counted rule — never to a
# client-supplied value.
# When UNSET: the gateway still rewrites (devices stay reachable) but stops
# forwarding the client IP, so gateway devices share Vercel's address as their
# throttle key. Set it. Must be >= 16 chars; generate with
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# GATEWAY_SHARED_SECRET=

# ─── Durable dashboard session (SEC-010, 2026-09-05) ───
# Set the SAME value on BOTH services (Vercel web + Railway API), or leave it
# unset on both. Must be >= 16 chars; generate with
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# What it protects: "Keep me logged in" used to mean a THIRTY-DAY access JWT in
# localStorage — JS-readable, so one XSS walked off with a month of access. The
# access token is now always <= 1h and the durable half is an opaque, single-use,
# rotating refresh secret in an HttpOnly cookie that page JavaScript cannot read.
# That cookie is FIRST-PARTY to the web origin (Safari's ITP drops the
# cross-site alternative outright), so the web app's own Node route handlers at
# /api/session/* hold it and call the API server-to-server. This secret is what
# proves that server-to-server caller really is the web origin's server.
#
# When UNSET: the endpoints still work (subtractive by design, like
# GATEWAY_SHARED_SECRET — a half-configured deploy must never sign every
# remembered operator out), but anything already holding a valid access token
# can mint a durable credential. That is no worse than the pre-SEC-010 posture,
# where a 30-day token was simply sitting in localStorage — but it does leave
# the last step of the finding open, so SET IT in production.
# SESSION_BFF_SECRET=

# ─── API Server ───
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=development

# ─── Outbound email (Resend) ───
# Powers EVERY outbound email: password-reset links, user invites, welcome
# mail, asset-approval notices, and the Bug Reporter's "we got it / fix
# shipped" + owner-alert emails. Get an API key at https://resend.com.
#
# When RESEND_API_KEY is UNSET:
#   - dev: emails are logged to the console instead of sent (zero-config).
#   - production: the API throws on every send so the UI surfaces a real
#     "email not configured" error instead of lying "check your inbox".
#
# WARNING — EMAIL_FROM and domain verification:
#   The default sender is "VenueOS <onboarding@resend.dev>". Resend only
#   delivers from onboarding@resend.dev to the EMAIL ADDRESS THAT OWNS THE
#   RESEND ACCOUNT — every other recipient is silently dropped / spam-
#   filtered. This is the usual cause of "I set the key but no bug emails
#   arrive." To send to anyone else (other admins, operators, parents) you
#   MUST verify a custom sending domain in the Resend dashboard and set
#   EMAIL_FROM to an address on that domain (e.g. "VenueOS <noreply@yourdomain.com>").
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM="VenueOS <onboarding@resend.dev>"
# Optional Reply-To header, e.g. a district IT help alias. Leave unset to omit.
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# ─── Onboarding emails ───
# Base URL used when generating password-reset and invite links in outbound
# emails. Should match the deployed web origin (e.g. https://edusignage.example).
APP_PUBLIC_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ─── Web → API base URL (REQUIRED in production) ────────────────────────────
# The browser bundle uses this to call the NestJS API. Without it, the
# deployed site falls back to http://localhost:8080 and every request fails
# with "Cannot connect to the server. Make sure the API is running."
#
# Local dev:   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/v1
# Production:  NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://<your-railway-app>.up.railway.app/api/v1
#
# On Vercel: Settings → Environment Variables → add NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL for
# Production/Preview/Development, paste the Railway public domain + /api/v1,
# then redeploy. Remember the trailing /api/v1 — the backend mounts all
# routes under that prefix.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/v1

# ─── Asset CDN (optional, default OFF) ──────────────────────────────────────
# When set, every Supabase Storage asset URL is rewritten to go through the
# Cloudflare Worker edge CDN proxy (apps/edge), which serves repeat fetches
# from Cloudflare's global cache instead of Supabase origin. This turns the
# 5+ GB/mo egress charge from Supabase into near-zero for hot assets.
#
# To enable:
#   1. Deploy the Cloudflare Worker (see apps/edge/README.md, "Asset CDN
#      deploy runbook" section added 2026-05-30).
#   2. Set ASSET_ORIGIN on the worker to your Supabase Storage base URL.
#   3. Set this variable to the worker's public URL, then redeploy the web app.
#
# Leave unset (or empty) in local dev and CI — assets load directly from
# Supabase with no CDN rewriting. Nothing breaks when this is unset.
#
# Example: NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_CDN=https://edu-cms-edge.<handle>.workers.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_CDN=

# ─── CSRF enforcement ───
# ENFORCE is now the default. The middleware blocks mutations with invalid
# or missing X-CSRF-Token headers unless you explicitly opt out:
#   CSRF_ENFORCE=false  → warn-only (logs, does not block)
#   CSRF_WARN=true      → warn-only (alias)
# Leave both unset in production.
# CSRF_ENFORCE=
# CSRF_WARN=

# ─── Dev-only WebSocket bypass ───
# When NODE_ENV!=production AND DEV_WS_ALLOW=true, the realtime gateway
# accepts unsigned `dev_<deviceId>_<tenantId>` tokens without JWT
# verification. NEVER set this in production — it bypasses all device
# auth on the signed-message channel.
# DEV_WS_ALLOW=

# ─── GrowthBook self-hosted feature flags ────────────────────────────────────
# Run `docker compose up growthbook` then open http://localhost:3100 to create
# an SDK Connection; paste the generated key below.
# Leave blank to fall back to the FF_* env-var switches below.
GROWTHBOOK_API_HOST=http://localhost:3100
GROWTHBOOK_CLIENT_KEY=                    # e.g. sdk-abc123

# Web-side GrowthBook (browser bundle). Must match the Server-Side SDK key
# for the same environment in GrowthBook, or a separate Browser SDK key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GROWTHBOOK_API_HOST=http://localhost:3100
NEXT_PUBLIC_GROWTHBOOK_CLIENT_KEY=        # e.g. sdk-abc123

# ─── Feature flag env-var fallback ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Used when GrowthBook is unreachable or keys are missing.
# Only exact value "true" enables a flag. (2026-08-05: the dead
# EMERGENCY_NEW_UI / SIS_INTEGRATION / TEMPLATE_BUILDER_V2 entries were
# removed — the first two gated nothing, and the builder kill-switch was
# deleted from code after a 'false' here silently disabled Customize.)
# API-side — server reads these.
FF_SPORTS_PLAYER_STATS=false
# Web-side — exposed to the browser bundle (requires rebuild to change).
NEXT_PUBLIC_FF_SPORTS_PLAYER_STATS=false

# ─── SSO (SAML + OIDC) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At-rest encryption key for tenant SSO secrets (x509 certs, OIDC client
# secrets). Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Without this the API falls back to a dev-only key and refuses to trust
# stored secrets across deploys — always set explicitly in production.
SSO_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Public base URL for the API (used to build SP metadata URLs for IdP setup).
# Optional — falls back to the incoming request's host/proto headers.
PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# Public URL for the web app (used to redirect back after SSO callback).
WEB_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ─── Sentry error tracking (free tier: https://sentry.io) ───
# Leave empty in dev if you don't have a DSN — app will run without it.
SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
GIT_COMMIT_SHA=

# ─── Clever SIS integration (Sprint 3) ───
# OAuth app credentials from https://dev.clever.com/ — obtain via sandbox account.
CLEVER_CLIENT_ID=
CLEVER_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Where Clever redirects after the OAuth consent screen. Must match the app config
# in the Clever developer dashboard. If unset, derived from request headers in dev.
CLEVER_REDIRECT_URI=
# Where the user is sent after a successful connect (relative URL on the web app).
CLEVER_POST_CONNECT_URL=/
# 64-char hex (32 bytes) — used for AES-256-GCM encryption of stored access tokens.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
CLEVER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Set to "1" to disable the nightly 02:00 roster sync cron (dev convenience).
CLEVER_CRON_DISABLED=

# ─── AI content generation (Anthropic Claude) ───
# Powers the "Generate" button next to text fields in the template
# editor (announcement / quote / promo / menu copy). Get a key at
# console.anthropic.com. When unset, the AI button surfaces a friendly
# "AI not configured for this deploy" error — feature degrades
# gracefully, the rest of the app keeps working. Cost cap: 30
# generations / hour / tenant via in-memory rate limit; claude-3-5-haiku
# at 300 max_tokens caps spend at ~$0.005 / call.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ─── Pexels free stock photography (IMAGERY wave, 2026-06-28) ───
# Makes EVERY photo-appropriate AI signage board come back with a real,
# relevant, gorgeous STOCK photo for $0 — for every tenant, regardless of
# which AI provider they use (the art-director resolves a Pexels photo at
# generate time and the engine art-directs it: brand grade + directional
# scrim). The operator can one-tap "Make it an AI photo" to upgrade to an
# on-brand AI-generated photo (that path uses their own OpenAI/Google key).
# When UNSET, boards simply ride their themed gradient exactly as before —
# the feature degrades gracefully and never throws on a stock miss. Free,
# generous tier (≈200 req/hour, 20k/month). Get a key (free, instant):
# pexels.com/api → "Get Started". Server-side only; never reaches the browser.
PEXELS_API_KEY=

# ─── Google Geocoding (address picker, 2026-05-31) ───
# Powers the screen-location + signup/onboarding address autocomplete via the
# server-side proxy GET /api/v1/geocode (the key NEVER ships to the browser).
# Google has authoritative US house-number coverage — it finds specific street
# addresses that the free OSM/Nominatim/Census geocoders miss. When UNSET, the
# picker falls back to OSM Nominatim automatically (street-level, free) — the
# app keeps working, just less precise on individual house numbers.
# Get a key: console.cloud.google.com → enable "Geocoding API" → Credentials →
# create API key → RESTRICT it to the Geocoding API (and your server IP if you
# can). Free tier ≈ 10,000 lookups/month; we only geocode at screen-setup time
# so real usage stays well under it. Billing must be enabled on the GCP project.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# ─── Square POS (Sprint 8d, 2026-05-25) ───
# OAuth app credentials from https://developer.squareup.com/apps. Sandbox
# accounts are free; sandbox + production endpoints are separate apps in
# Square's dashboard. The OAuth client_id + secret go below.
#
# To enable Square catalog sync end-to-end:
#   1. Sign up at developer.squareup.com (free; sandbox merchant included).
#   2. Create an OAuth app. Add redirect URI:
#        https://<your-api>/api/v1/pos/oauth/square/callback
#   3. Subscribe a webhook to `catalog.version.updated` +
#      `inventory.count.updated`. Notification URL:
#        https://<your-api>/api/v1/pos/webhook/square
#      Copy the signature key into SQUARE_WEBHOOK_SIG_KEY below.
#   4. Set SQUARE_ENV=production when ready; leave unset / "sandbox" for dev.
SQUARE_CLIENT_ID=
SQUARE_CLIENT_SECRET=
SQUARE_WEBHOOK_SIG_KEY=
SQUARE_ENV=sandbox

# ─── Clover POS (self-serve, 2026-06-02) ───
# Free sandbox at https://sandbox.dev.clover.com. Create an app in the
# Clover dashboard, add redirect URI:
#   https://<your-api>/api/v1/pos/oauth/clover/callback
# Leave CLOVER_ENV unset / "sandbox" for dev; set "production" at go-live.
CLOVER_CLIENT_ID=
CLOVER_CLIENT_SECRET=
CLOVER_ENV=sandbox

# ─── Lightspeed Retail (X-Series) POS (self-serve, 2026-06-02) ───
# Free sandbox + OAuth at https://x-series-api.lightspeedhq.com. Create an
# app, add redirect URI:
#   https://<your-api>/api/v1/pos/oauth/lightspeed-retail/callback
# Leave LIGHTSPEED_ENV unset / "sandbox" for dev; "production" at go-live.
LIGHTSPEED_CLIENT_ID=
LIGHTSPEED_CLIENT_SECRET=
LIGHTSPEED_ENV=sandbox

# ─── Shopify POS (self-serve, 2026-06-02) ───
# Free Partner account + dev store at https://partners.shopify.com. Create
# an app, add redirect URI:
#   https://<your-api>/api/v1/pos/oauth/shopify-pos/callback
# The operator enters their shop domain (acme.myshopify.com) at connect time.
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─── Canva Connect (NOT built yet — Sprint 11; Stage-2 design imports) ───
# Apply at canva.dev/docs/connect. The live "Sign in with Canva" OAuth flow
# is not implemented yet, so setting these alone does NOT enable a working
# Canva import. Today: export your Canva design to PDF / PNG and drop it at
# /[schoolId]/templates/imports — that works without these keys.
CANVA_CLIENT_ID=
CANVA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Proxy render capability (SEC-006, 2026-09-04) ───────────────────────────
# Signs the short-lived, URL-bound capability that authorises ONE Chromium
# render. `GET /api/v1/proxy/web` stays public (it is an iframe src and cannot
# carry a bearer token), but its Chromium half no longer is: `render()` needs a
# grant, and the only source of one is `POST /api/v1/proxy/render-capability`,
# which sits behind JwtAuthGuard. A missing/expired capability DEGRADES to the
# safeFetch strip-scripts path — it never blanks a screen.
# Unset => derives from DEVICE_SECRET_KEY (works; shares blast radius).
# PROXY_RENDER_SECRET=

# Escape hatch for the gate above: `1` lets UNAUTHENTICATED callers drive
# Chromium again (pre-SEC-006 behaviour) and logs a warning. Anything else,
# including a typo, leaves the gate closed. Leave unset.
# PROXY_SSR_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS=

# ── Dashboard CSP script-src (SEC-010, 2026-09-04) ──────────────────────────
# `off` / `0` / `false` / `report` makes the middleware emit NO CSP header,
# reverting to the pre-SEC-010 report-only policy with 'unsafe-inline'.
# Default (unset) = ENFORCED, nonce-based, on every dynamically rendered
# dashboard route. Read at build time.
# CSP_SCRIPT_SRC_ENFORCE=

# ── Player trust anchor (security wave 2026-08-01, finding R-01) ────────────
# Optional. Extra API hosts the PLAYER may be pointed at via ?api=.
# The player validates any ?api= override (and the stored value on read)
# against: NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL's host, the page origin, a built-in known-good
# set, and this list. https only; exact host or dot-boundary suffix match.
# Leave UNSET unless you run a staging / on-prem install that points kiosks at
# a host outside NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL — an unlisted host is now refused.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ROOT_ALLOWLIST=https://staging-api.yourdomain.com
